.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/eslint-visitor-keys Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "eslint-visitor-keys"
.PP
\fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP

.PP
Constants and utilities about visitor keys to traverse AST\&.
.SH "💿 Installation"
.PP
Use \fRnpm\fP to install\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install eslint\-visitor\-keys
.fi
.PP
.SS "Requirements"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRNode\&.js\fP \fR^18\&.18\&.0\fP, \fR^20\&.9\&.0\fP, or \fR>=21\&.1\&.0\fP
.PP
.SH "📖 Usage"
.PP
To use in an ESM file:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import * as evk from "eslint\-visitor\-keys"
.fi
.PP

.PP
To use in a CommonJS file:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const evk = require("eslint\-visitor\-keys")
.fi
.PP
.SS "evk\&.KEYS"
.RS 4
type: \fR{ [type: string]: string[] | undefined }\fP 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Visitor keys\&. This keys are frozen\&.

.PP
This is an object\&. Keys are the type of \fRESTree\fP nodes\&. Their values are an array of property names which have child nodes\&.

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(evk\&.KEYS\&.AssignmentExpression) // → ["left", "right"]
.fi
.PP
.SS "evk\&.getKeys(node)"
.RS 4
type: \fR(node: object) => string[]\fP 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Get the visitor keys of a given AST node\&.

.PP
This is similar to \fRObject\&.keys(node)\fP of ES Standard, but some keys are excluded: \fRparent\fP, \fRleadingComments\fP, \fRtrailingComments\fP, and names which start with \fR_\fP\&.

.PP
This will be used to traverse unknown nodes\&.

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const node = {
    type: "AssignmentExpression",
    left: { type: "Identifier", name: "foo" },
    right: { type: "Literal", value: 0 }
}
console\&.log(evk\&.getKeys(node)) // → ["type", "left", "right"]
.fi
.PP
.SS "evk\&.unionWith(additionalKeys)"
.RS 4
type: \fR(additionalKeys: object) => { [type: string]: string[] | undefined }\fP 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Make the union set with \fRevk\&.KEYS\fP and the given keys\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
The order of keys is, \fRadditionalKeys\fP is at first, then \fRevk\&.KEYS\fP is concatenated after that\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
It removes duplicated keys as keeping the first one\&.
.PP

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(evk\&.unionWith({
    MethodDefinition: ["decorators"]
})) // → { \&.\&.\&., MethodDefinition: ["decorators", "key", "value"], \&.\&.\&. }
.fi
.PP
